# Warning Text

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Place a short warning before an action that can cause a loss or change that is often missed. Name the consequence and the thing it affects, in plain words.

A warning has two forms. Warning text is one bold sentence with an exclamation mark, placed directly above the action. A warning callout is a yellow box with a heading and a few sentences, used in reference content to correct a likely mistake.

Home Assistant has no warning text element, so this is a Civica pattern. On a dashboard, its nearest native piece is the warning alert described under Variants. This page owns both forms.

This page covers:

- Warning Text
  - Civica Warning Text Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

Warning text has an exclamation mark in a dark circle and a bold sentence. The exclamation mark is only decoration. A hidden word, “Warning”, comes first in the text, so screen readers say it and people who cannot see the mark still get the meaning.

A warning callout has a pale yellow box with a thin border, a heading and a paragraph. A hidden word, “Important:”, comes first in the heading when it adds meaning.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-warning-text-pattern">Civica Warning Text Pattern</h3>

**Warning text** goes directly before the action it concerns, in reading order as well as on screen. One warning goes with one decision.

**A warning callout** needs a specific heading and a self-contained explanation. Use it sparingly, when missing the information could lead to a significant mistake. Do not make every helpful note yellow, and do not use it instead of inline form validation. For a Home Assistant home, it suits two cases: making clear that an unavailable reading is not a confirmed physical state, and saying that an import may replace existing settings. Do not invent alarm advice or device operating instructions. Link to the verified guidance that owns the topic.

**Colours.** The callout uses a fixed pale yellow fill with near-black text, so it does not change with the theme’s primary colour or with dark mode. On a dashboard, the Civica theme has two warning colours for a solid yellow surface: `civica-warning-fill` (#FFDD00) and `civica-on-warning` (#0B0C0C), the same in light and dark mode. Calculated from those two colours, the text is about 14.6 to 1 on the fill. The theme’s other warning colours, `warning-color` and `ha-color-on-warning-normal`, change with the mode.

**Home Assistant alternatives.**

- The nearest native piece is `ha-alert` with `alert-type="warning"`. In a Markdown card, write a quote that starts with `[!WARNING]`. See [Alert & Tip Callout](alert-and-tip-callout.html). A native alert announces itself at once, so use it for a message that appears after an action, not for a static warning that was there from the start.
- A Civica Markdown card with the yellow surface is the [Emergency banner](../publishing/components/emergency-banner.html). It needs the card-mod integration.

Neither stops a harmful action. A warning does not make anything safe, and a device’s own limits stay the job of the device and its integration.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The word “Warning” or “Important:” is text, so meaning does not depend on the icon or the colour.
- A warning before an action is read before the action, in the right order.
- A warning is not a confirmation. Home Assistant’s own confirmations stay, and the real action needs its own permission and failure handling.
- The callout colours are fixed, so on a dashboard they do not follow the theme.
- Too many warnings stop being read.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a warning for a meaningful change, such as removing a dashboard or replacing a configuration. Do not use it for routine information, a successful action or every unavailable sensor.

Name the actual consequence: “Removing this dashboard deletes its saved layout. It does not remove devices from Home Assistant.” Avoid capital letters and vague questions such as “Are you sure?”. Use plain verbs. Keep the cancel route clear. Do not style a destructive action like the ordinary primary action only because the theme colour is Green or Red.

When the decision needs more explanation, use the [interruption pattern](../patterns/interruption.html). For wording, see [States, requests and errors](../content/writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html). For the actions that follow a warning, see [Button](buttons.html).

Before you publish one, check that the warning comes before the action in visual and reading order. Test it at 400% zoom, with a screen reader and in forced colours. Use the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html).

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the warning is real text before the action.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the word carries the meaning, not the icon or the yellow.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: calculated ratios are in the Colours paragraph. Check your own theme.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: the text wraps and the box grows.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: a callout heading says what the callout is about.
- 3.3.4 Error Prevention: a warning before a change does not replace a confirmation.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: a warning that appears after an action needs to be announced. A static warning does not.

One consequence, one decision and one clear way back keep the effort low.

